{
  "dom_id": "step-e3224",
  "name": "Example: Finding the Equation of a Circle Given the Center and the Radius",
  "problem": "What is the equation of the circle given below?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13957423/assets/79111226781bc9d3ea9c00e217b93b9661d1e70bdfbee688c555dd899cf3d80e.png)",
  "worked_solution": "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is \n\n$$\n(x-a)^2 + (y-b)^2 = r^2.\n$$\n\nFrom the picture, we see that our circle is centered at $C(-1,1)$ and the radius of the circle is $r = 3.$ Therefore, the equation of the circle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-(-1))^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & ={3}^{2} \\\\ (x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "result": null,
